11.0.0, sixth TestFlight upload — the app survives what a device does to it

What is new since 10008. Each line is code that the Echoel Studio run builds and, where a test
target reaches it, tests; nothing here is device-verified yet.
- A song is one file in Files (type com.echoelmusic.song, extension .echoel). A song tapped in
  Files or received by AirDrop opens in Echoel and joins the song list.
- Songs are saved in format 2: a song saved by this build does NOT open in builds 10004–10008.
- A broken song file is refused whole instead of opening half.
- When the newest song cannot be opened, the newest one that can comes up and the status line
  names the one that did not. It stays in the list.
- When the last launch did not get past its first seconds, the app starts a new song instead of
  reopening the same one, and says so. Every song stays in the list. The song that was opening
  then does not open by itself again until it is chosen in the song list; the status line names
  it.
- An import of earlier songs that was running when a launch died is not tried again, and the
  status line says so; a damaged earlier song with an out-of-range velocity imports instead of
  stopping the app.
- A failed audio start is tried again; after three tries Play becomes Restart audio. Play also
  rebuilds the audio when iOS stopped it without telling.
- Unplugging headphones pauses the song. A call, a media reset or an external clock's Stop ends
  a running take and keeps what it captured.
- A plugin whose extension crashes is loaded again.
- A new sample rate (an interface plugged in) keeps the playhead and the plugin settings.
- The mod wheel, expression and any knob that is not learned play the live instrument and are
  recorded in a take; a learned one moves only its parameter. A hosted plugin instrument gets
  every controller. The Echoel Synth Audio Unit reads the pitch wheel, the pedal and the mod
  wheel; panic stops the drums.
- Deleting a playing track or changing its instrument fades out; a stolen sampler voice, an EQ
  band coming back and the plugin demo's silence fade instead of clicking.
- A tempo change while playing never loses a note-off and crossfades audio tracks.
- The assistant shows only on a device that can run its model.
- New Song and Open keep the built-in effects' settings made since the app last left the
  foreground; before, a switch from the song menu dropped them.
- Soloing a return track plays it with what the other tracks send to it, instead of silence.
- Tapping a clip in Arrange opens that clip; a recorded MIDI take can now be edited.
- Export WAV is offered only when something is on the timeline, so it never shares an empty file.
- Every automatic audio recovery reopens the input an armed audio track asked for, and says so
  when it cannot; Play no longer runs the song over audio that failed to come back.
- Paused from outside while in the background, the app stops its audio instead of staying up
  silent; following a DAW's clock, it comes back after a call in the background.
- A MIDI take ended by a sample-rate change keeps its notes in time.
- After a launch that did not finish, the new song is not saved until it is edited, so the
  next launch opens your newest song again. A song received, or copied in with the Files app,
  under that unsaved song's name is not overwritten by it.
- Record and a session launch start nothing while the audio cannot run, even while the song
  shows as playing; a new take waits until the last one is written out.
- Deleting the armed track, or arming an instrument track instead, closes the input it opened.
- When the input cannot be opened again while an audio take records, the take ends there and
  keeps what it recorded, in time; the message says why the input failed. Opening the input for
  a monitor set to in ends a running take first, since it stops the song.
- During MIDI Learn knobs, the pitch wheel, pressure, program change and a pressed pedal play
  nothing, so a plugin cannot take the learn; notes, panic and All Notes Off play, and a held
  wheel or pedal can return to rest.
- A plugin that stopped is named in the status line once it is loaded again, or with the reason
  it is not; several are all named. Notices no longer hide each other, and an export clears
  only the ones that stood before it.
- The status line sits under the transport strip instead of over its buttons, and the launch
  messages stay until something replaces them instead of going with the first autosave.

Previous: 11.0.0 (10008), fifth upload — the app records the plugin purchase in the App Group
group.com.echoelmusic.shared and the Audio Units read it there (factory job 002). Before that:
10007 (finding no earlier songs no longer marks the import done), 10006, 10005, 10004 (the first
Echoel Studio build). group.com.echoelmusic (the import of earlier songs) is still not requested
(F17): until it is, the import finds nothing.

Bump this file on a commit whose "Echoel Studio" run is green to upload a build
(.github/workflows/studio-testflight.yml). Build number = 10000 + the workflow's run number.
The version is MARKETING_VERSION in EchoelStudio/project.yml.
This file is a test list for the device, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

Five things to try on the device (FOUNDER_INBOX rule 1: five per build; answer each yes or no):
1. Plugin purchase family SP1-SP3 (FOUNDER_INBOX section 2): load Echoel Synth, Drums and EQ in
   AUM with 10007 (the baseline), then with this build — no -3000 — and without a purchase the
   Synth pauses for 2 s after 28 s, every 30 s.
2. Files app -> On My iPhone -> Echoel: each song is ONE file, not a folder. Share or AirDrop one
   to yourself and tap it: it opens in Echoel and appears once in the song list.
3. Arm an instrument track and move a MIDI keyboard's mod wheel: vibrato. MIDI-learn the wheel to
   a parameter: from then on it moves only that parameter.
4. Play with headphones and unplug them: the song pauses and the status line says why. Record a
   take and take a call: the take ends and is kept, and after the call Play plays.
5. Record a take on an armed audio track: Stop fades it out and its reverb rings. Export a song
   with a long reverb: the file ends after the tail, not 2 s after the last note.
